    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.add_visibility_graph - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.add_visibility_graph.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_visibility_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add the natural visibility graph of a time series.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_visibility_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add the natural visibility graph of a time series.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.add_visibility_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Add the natural visibility graph of a time series.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nAdd the natural visibility graph of a time series.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L18572-L18707){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.add_visibility_graph(\n    series,\n    value_attr=\"value\",\n    type=None,\n    label=True,\n    rel=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Visibility graph](../assets/graphs/add-visibility-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Three natural visibility graphs of short time series, each value drawn as a dashed stem rising from a baseline with its node on top. Two values are joined when the straight line between their tops clears every value in between, so neighbors in time are always joined and a tall value such as the 5 in the last series sees far in both directions.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nCreates one node per value of `series`, in order, and joins two time points when they\ncan \"see\" each other: drawing the series as bars, two bar tops are joined if the\nstraight line between them passes strictly above every bar in between. Neighboring\npoints always see each other, so the graph contains the path through the series, and\npeaks see far, so they become hubs. The construction (Lacasa et al. 2008) turns a time\nseries into a network whose structure reflects the series: periodic series give\nregular graphs, random ones exponential degree distributions, and fractal ones power\nlaws. It is NetworkX's `visibility_graph()`.\n\nA point exactly on the line blocks the view, as in NetworkX. Slopes are compared by\ncross-multiplication, which is exact for integer values; NetworkX computes the line\nand can round the other way when integer points are exactly collinear. Each node's\nvalue is stored in the `value_attr=` node attribute. In a directed graph every edge\npoints forward in time. The graph is appended without touching or connecting to\nexisting nodes, and the whole construction is recorded as a single step.\n\nVisibility graphs are useful for:\n\n- analyzing time series with network measures (degree distribution, clustering,\n  motifs), e.g. to tell chaotic from random signals\n- detecting peaks and regime changes, since high points get high degree\n- comparing series through the networks they generate\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[series]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The time series, as a list of finite numbers.\n\n<code>[value_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;value&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of the node attribute that stores each node's value. Default is `\"value\"`.\n    Pass `None` to skip storing it.\n\n<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),\n    no `type` attribute is set.\n\n<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; a label\n    that is already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, the\n    nodes are left unlabelled.\n\n<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),\n    no `rel` attribute is set.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If a value is not a finite number.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~add_interval_graph`) \n\n:   Build a graph from overlapping intervals.\n\n[](`~add_path_graph`) \n\n:   Build the path that a steadily rising series gives.\n\n[](`~get_node_df`) \n\n:   See each point's stored value next to its degree.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe peak at the third position sees everything up to the `3`, which hides the last\npoint from it, while the low points only see their two neighbors:\n\n::: {#d60f5218 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_visibility_graph([2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 1])\n\ngraph.get_edges_str_repr()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['1--2', '1--3', '2--3', '3--4', '3--5', '4--5', '5--6']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEach node keeps its value, and the peak has the highest degree:\n\n::: {#e06b2ee8 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph.get_node_df().select(\"label\", \"value\", \"degree\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (6, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>label</th><th>value</th><th>degree</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>i64</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;1&quot;</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;2&quot;</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;3&quot;</td><td>5</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;4&quot;</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;5&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;6&quot;</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A valley or a hill\n\nSeven values each, drawn at their position and height. In the valley on the left every\npoint looks over the lower ones between, so all `21` pairs see each other. On the hill on\nthe right the middle points block the view, and only the `6` neighboring pairs remain, a\npath. A straight line is a path in the same way:\n\n::: {#cb1578f3 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndef drawn(series):\n    graph = ec.Graph().add_visibility_graph(series)\n    values = graph.get_node_attrs(\"value\")\n    graph.set_node_attrs(\"pos\", {v: (v, y) for v, y in values.items()})\n    return graph\n\nvalley = drawn([7, 4, 2, 1, 2, 4, 7])\nhill = drawn([1, 4, 6, 7, 6, 4, 1])\n\nvalley.count_edges(), hill.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(21, 6)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#bbf66003 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nvalley.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"value\",\n    width=320, height=320, legend=False,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,